{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NfxKzhhkhqs2PTMtub8ZPV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Conventions\n2\t\n3\tThe standards the code of 495 meets. `AGENTS.md` (symlinked as `CLAUDE.md`) carries the project\n4\tspine — stack, commands, architecture, never-do list. How a change is made — the six steps, the\n5\treview, the acceptance run, the landing, the commit messages — is `cycle/README.md`.\n6\t\n7\t## Preflight\n8\t\n9\t**Preflight** is `npm run check`: typecheck, the test suite and the `lint:*` scripts chained\n10\ttogether, under Node 24, the declared floor (`specs/adr/D-50`). It is green before any forward work,\n11\tnot \"green enough for this task\". A defect costs roughly 1× to fix in development, 10× in\n12\tintegration, 100× in production: fix a red gate now.\n13\t\n14\t| Control | Refuses |\n15\t|---------|---------|\n16\t| `lint:code` | what Biome refuses (`specs/adr/D-42`, `D-43`) |\n17\t| `lint:layers` | an import against the direction of `AGENTS.md` § Architecture |\n18\t| `lint:architecture` | a `CMP-*` id claimed in `src/` without a row in `specs/archive/amont/conception-technique.md` §4.1, or an import cycle |\n19\t| `lint:exports` | an export nothing reads outside its module (`specs/adr/D-44`) |\n20\t| `lint:traceability` | a `[P0]` requirement of `specs/archive/amont/expression-besoins.md` absent from `specs/archive/TRACEABILITY.md` |\n21\t| `lint:declarations`, `lint:distribution` | a published surface that drifts from `src/` |\n22\t| `lint:story-format` | a story of `specs/stories/` that departs from `cycle/format-de-story.md` |\n23\t\n24\t## Code Style\n25\t\n26\t- One thing per function, one responsibility per module (SRP).\n27\t- Prefer small, focused modules over god files. Split a file when it grows a second responsibility.\n28\t- Give every name a specific, unique meaning. Avoid `data`, `handler`, `Manager`, `Service`.\n29\t- Type everything explicitly. Never use `any`. Never leave a public function untyped.\n30\t- Extract shared logic into one function or module. Never duplicate logic.\n31\t- Prefer an early return over a nested `if`.\n32\t- Throw an exception instead of returning an error code or a boolean sentinel.\n33\t- Delete dead code. Never comment it out — git history already holds it.\n34\t- Boy Scout Rule: leave every file you touch at least as clean as you found it.\n35\t- Write the minimum code that solves the stated problem. No preventive abstraction, no unused\n36\t  configuration layer. Inspect what the dependencies already do before adding a package or writing\n37\t  a capability a maintained library provides.\n38\t\n39\t## Comments\n40\t\n41\t- Keep existing comments on a refactor. They carry intent and provenance.\n42\t- Write why, never what. The code already says what it does.\n43\t- Never write an obvious comment that restates the code.\n44\t- Never leave commented-out code. Delete it and rely on git history.\n45\t- Describe the behavior of the code, never the process that produced it: no ticket, story, review\n46\t  round or session reference in a comment.\n47\t\n48\t## Tests (F.I.R.S.T)\n49\t\n50\t- Run the whole suite headless with one command: `npm test`.\n51\t- Give every new function a test. Give every bug fix a regression test.\n52\t- Name a fake used for external I/O as a fake class, not an inline stub.\n53\t- Keep every test Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-Validating, and Timely.\n54\t- Never skip or ignore a test without a written note on what stays unresolved.\n55\t- Test every boundary condition: empty input, maximum, minimum, and the off-by-one case.\n56\t- Assert only through the public interface — return values, contracts, view state. Never assert on\n57\t  private state.\n58\t- A test is written before the code it pins, and fails on the assertion the story states before\n59\t  that code exists (`cycle/README.md` § Les six pas).\n60\t\n61\t## Dependencies\n62\t\n63\t- Inject a dependency through a constructor or parameter. Never reach for a global or a bare import.\n64\t- Wrap a third-party library behind a project-owned port (see `src/ports/`).\n65\t- Keep a dependency at its latest published version whenever Preflight stays green; a caret on a\n66\t  `0.x` version silently locks the minor. `@types/node` tracks `engines.node`, not the newest\n67\t  release (`specs/adr/D-50`).\n68\t\n69\t## Pi is the host, not one dependency among others\n70\t\n71\t495 is an extension of Pi. Pi is what keeps 495 from rebuilding what already exists, and what lets\n72\tit state a fact instead of inferring one. Reach for Pi's own API first — before writing the\n73\tcapability, and before deducing from the outside what Pi can report from the inside\n74\t(`specs/adr/D-55`).\n75\t\n76\t- **Look for the API before building.** A hook, an event, a runtime or a typed result Pi already\n77\t  publishes beats a table 495 maintains, a file 495 parses, or a fact 495 reads out of a package's\n78\t  code.\n79\t- **Read the documentation the pinned package ships.** It is already on disk, at the exact version\n80\t  installed: `node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/`, and `extensions.md` there carries\n81\t  the event list. Prefer it to any URL: <https://pi.dev/docs/latest> tracks the newest Pi, not the\n82\t  one this repository has.\n83\t- **Confirm in the pinned surface before building on it.** `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/**/*.d.ts`\n84\t  and the peer packages say what the release ships. What is built on an API names the version it\n85\t  was confirmed against.\n86\t- **Prefer an observed fact to a restated one.** What Pi hands over was measured; what 495 restates\n87\t  is believed, and drifts the day the host changes. Say in the code which of the two a value is.\n88\t- **A workaround names what it replaces.** When no API covers the need, write where the workaround\n89\t  lives what was looked for and not found, so it can be deleted the day it arrives.\n90\t- **Leaning on Pi never bends the layering.** The Pi API is reached through `extension/` or\n91\t  `adapters/pi-worker/` and nowhere else (`AGENTS.md` § Architecture).\n92\t\n93\t## Structure\n94\t\n95\t- Follow the layering declared in `AGENTS.md` § Architecture.\n96\t- Keep paths predictable: one adapter per external system, one port per capability.\n97\t\n98\t## Formatting\n99\t\n100\t- Lint and format with Biome (`npm run lint:code`) and type with `tsc --noEmit`. No style debates\n101\t  beyond that.\n102\t- The formatter runs at 120 columns. Import order is not machine-sorted: imports are grouped by the\n103\t  architectural layer they come from, which is how the one-way dependency direction stays readable.\n104\t\n105\t## Logging\n106\t\n107\t- Emit structured, redaction-aware output through the existing presentation layer.\n108\t- Never print a raw secret, token, or unredacted workspace path to a shared channel.\n109\t\n110\t## Defensive Code\n111\t\n112\t- **Retry** — bounded retry already backs execution, the Pi worker, and git integration\n113\t  (`src/adapters/execution/`, `src/adapters/pi-worker/`, `src/adapters/git/integrator.ts`).\n114\t- **Timeout** — every sandboxed process and agent session runs under a budget\n115\t  (`src/adapters/sandbox/process.ts`, `src/application/harness.ts`, `policy.budgets.intervention_ms`).\n116\t- **Graceful degradation** — an unqualified capability refuses with `capability_missing` instead of\n117\t  running unconfined (`src/domain/errors.ts`).\n118\t- **Circuit breaker** — a change's retry budget is bounded; once exhausted, the gate refuses further\n119\t  automatic retries and asks a human to resume or decide (`src/domain/gates/g2.ts`,\n120\t  `src/domain/change/decide.ts`).\n121\t\n122\tThe agent implements defensive code only for the categories listed here.\n123\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36071154-c7ac-4982-b9ec-3fc001e44241","uuid":"441a338f-9fe4-4bc5-b9ea-bf8cb4c5ef0b","timestamp":"2026-09-29T18:27:18.940Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jeanjerome/Projets/495-pi-package/CONVENTIONS.md","content":"# Conventions\n\nThe standards the code of 495 meets. `AGENTS.md` (symlinked as `CLAUDE.md`) carries the project\nspine — stack, commands, architecture, never-do list. How a change is made — the six steps, the\nreview, the acceptance run, the landing, the commit messages — is `cycle/README.md`.\n\n## Preflight\n\n**Preflight** is `npm run check`: typecheck, the test suite and the `lint:*` scripts chained\ntogether, under Node 24, the declared floor (`specs/adr/D-50`). It is green before any forward work,\nnot \"green enough for this task\". A defect costs roughly 1× to fix in development, 10× in\nintegration, 100× in production: fix a red gate now.\n\n| Control | Refuses |\n|---------|---------|\n| `lint:code` | what Biome refuses (`specs/adr/D-42`, `D-43`) |\n| `lint:layers` | an import against the direction of `AGENTS.md` § Architecture |\n| `lint:architecture` | a `CMP-*` id claimed in `src/` without a row in `specs/archive/amont/conception-technique.md` §4.1, or an import cycle |\n| `lint:exports` | an export nothing reads outside its module (`specs/adr/D-44`) |\n| `lint:traceability` | a `[P0]` requirement of `specs/archive/amont/expression-besoins.md` absent from `specs/archive/TRACEABILITY.md` |\n| `lint:declarations`, `lint:distribution` | a published surface that drifts from `src/` |\n| `lint:story-format` | a story of `specs/stories/` that departs from `cycle/format-de-story.md` |\n\n## Code Style\n\n- One thing per function, one responsibility per module (SRP).\n- Prefer small, focused modules over god files. Split a file when it grows a second responsibility.\n- Give every name a specific, unique meaning. Avoid `data`, `handler`, `Manager`, `Service`.\n- Type everything explicitly. Never use `any`. Never leave a public function untyped.\n- Extract shared logic into one function or module. Never duplicate logic.\n- Prefer an early return over a nested `if`.\n- Throw an exception instead of returning an error code or a boolean sentinel.\n- Delete dead code. Never comment it out — git history already holds it.\n- Boy Scout Rule: leave every file you touch at least as clean as you found it.\n- Write the minimum code that solves the stated problem. No preventive abstraction, no unused\n  configuration layer. Inspect what the dependencies already do before adding a package or writing\n  a capability a maintained library provides.\n\n## Comments\n\n- Keep existing comments on a refactor. They carry intent and provenance.\n- Write why, never what. The code already says what it does.\n- Never write an obvious comment that restates the code.\n- Never leave commented-out code. Delete it and rely on git history.\n- Describe the behavior of the code, never the process that produced it: no ticket, story, review\n  round or session reference in a comment.\n\n## Tests (F.I.R.S.T)\n\n- Run the whole suite headless with one command: `npm test`.\n- Give every new function a test. Give every bug fix a regression test.\n- Name a fake used for external I/O as a fake class, not an inline stub.\n- Keep every test Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-Validating, and Timely.\n- Never skip or ignore a test without a written note on what stays unresolved.\n- Test every boundary condition: empty input, maximum, minimum, and the off-by-one case.\n- Assert only through the public interface — return values, contracts, view state. Never assert on\n  private state.\n- A test is written before the code it pins, and fails on the assertion the story states before\n  that code exists (`cycle/README.md` § Les six pas).\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Inject a dependency through a constructor or parameter. Never reach for a global or a bare import.\n- Wrap a third-party library behind a project-owned port (see `src/ports/`).\n- Keep a dependency at its latest published version whenever Preflight stays green; a caret on a\n  `0.x` version silently locks the minor. `@types/node` tracks `engines.node`, not the newest\n  release (`specs/adr/D-50`).\n\n## Pi is the host, not one dependency among others\n\n495 is an extension of Pi. Pi is what keeps 495 from rebuilding what already exists, and what lets\nit state a fact instead of inferring one. Reach for Pi's own API first — before writing the\ncapability, and before deducing from the outside what Pi can report from the inside\n(`specs/adr/D-55`).\n\n- **Look for the API before building.** A hook, an event, a runtime or a typed result Pi already\n  publishes beats a table 495 maintains, a file 495 parses, or a fact 495 reads out of a package's\n  code.\n- **Read the documentation the pinned package ships.** It is already on disk, at the exact version\n  installed: `node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/`, and `extensions.md` there carries\n  the event list. Prefer it to any URL: <https://pi.dev/docs/latest> tracks the newest Pi, not the\n  one this repository has.\n- **Confirm in the pinned surface before building on it.** `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/**/*.d.ts`\n  and the peer packages say what the release ships. What is built on an API names the version it\n  was confirmed against.\n- **Prefer an observed fact to a restated one.** What Pi hands over was measured; what 495 restates\n  is believed, and drifts the day the host changes. Say in the code which of the two a value is.\n- **A workaround names what it replaces.** When no API covers the need, write where the workaround\n  lives what was looked for and not found, so it can be deleted the day it arrives.\n- **Leaning on Pi never bends the layering.** The Pi API is reached through `extension/` or\n  `adapters/pi-worker/` and nowhere else (`AGENTS.md` § Architecture).\n\n## Structure\n\n- Follow the layering declared in `AGENTS.md` § Architecture.\n- Keep paths predictable: one adapter per external system, one port per capability.\n\n## Formatting\n\n- Lint and format with Biome (`npm run lint:code`) and type with `tsc --noEmit`. No style debates\n  beyond that.\n- The formatter runs at 120 columns. Import order is not machine-sorted: imports are grouped by the\n  architectural layer they come from, which is how the one-way dependency direction stays readable.\n\n## Logging\n\n- Emit structured, redaction-aware output through the existing presentation layer.\n- Never print a raw secret, token, or unredacted workspace path to a shared channel.\n\n## Defensive Code\n\n- **Retry** — bounded retry already backs execution, the Pi worker, and git integration\n  (`src/adapters/execution/`, `src/adapters/pi-worker/`, `src/adapters/git/integrator.ts`).\n- **Timeout** — every sandboxed process and agent session runs under a budget\n  (`src/adapters/sandbox/process.ts`, `src/application/harness.ts`, `policy.budgets.intervention_ms`).\n- **Graceful degradation** — an unqualified capability refuses with `capability_missing` instead of\n  running unconfined (`src/domain/errors.ts`).\n- **Circuit breaker** — a change's retry budget is bounded; once exhausted, the gate refuses further\n  automatic retries and asks a human to resume or decide (`src/domain/gates/g2.ts`,\n  `src/domain/change/decide.ts`).\n\nThe agent implements defensive code only for the categories listed here.\n","numLines":123,"startLine":1,"totalLines":123}}}
